# Design yardsticks (owner directives, Oct 2026)

- **Minimal copy is the yardstick.** As little copy as possible: no
  label-above-the-label, no eyebrow + title + description stacks, no hero
  that restates the page's own name. Pages start at their content. If a
  line of copy doesn't change what the user does next, cut it.
- **Muse (muse.ai) is the primary design-system / IA inspiration**,
  tailored to BotBound: calm, few words, one obvious next action.
- **One pattern per job.** One marketing footer (MarketingChromeBar), one
  workspace page header (WorkspaceShell title + action), one route
  fallback, one skeleton, one empty state. Do not add a parallel variant —
  extend the existing component. The CSS "polish/pass/hotfix" layers are
  debt: consolidate components first; CSS burn-down happens in its own car,
  never mixed into feature work.
- **Surfaces are sized for BotBound's actual accounts**: personal = 1 bot,
  SMB = 1–few bots. Fleet chrome (rankings, density grids, attribution
  for accounts with no campaigns) erodes trust — don't build it.
- Headline features must be *shown working*, not just named: a capability
  tab/panel carries a real example of the work, not a slogan.
- Loading states: shared progress sweep / skeletons only. Never render
  status sentences ("X loaded", "Loading page") as page content.
- Contract tests pin copy and structure. When a change alters pinned copy,
  update the pinning test in the same PR and say so in the PR body.
